// Variant: Null & Orange (3 Suits). 3 players, our_player_index=0.

// Pace 0 opens section 4 only when Alice is FORCED -- a known play still wins.
//
// The companion to DecisionMaking1973996. Section 4 lives in decision phase 1,
// which sits ABOVE the play phase, and its floor returns some clue regardless of
// tier. So "at pace 0" on its own would make Alice clue instead of playing a
// card she can already see, which is backwards at a pace where a play is the
// only thing that scores. DECISION_MAKING.md section 4 says "locked, at 8
// clues, or at pace 0 **and is forced to clue or pitch**", and the forced
// clause is what this pins.
//
// The first cut dropped it. Swept over the 1187 logged pace-0 turns that moved
// 272, and 133 of them were `play -> clue`. With the clause restored the same
// sweep moves 135, of which 130 are the intended `discard -> clue`; the five
// remaining `play -> clue` all give up a PITCH or a blind gamble rather than a
// known play, which is what the rung is for.
//
// Replay 1943094 T19, `Null & Orange (3 Suits)`: stacks [2,2,3], five cards
// left, three players -- pace 0. will-bot67's slot 5 is inferred to exactly
// {r3}, and red is on 2, so she KNOWS it plays. She must play it.
